L000174	\begin{abstract}
L000175	
L000176	\noindent We show that the tree-level spectrum of heavy particles can be directly extracted from the Wilson coefficients of the corresponding effective field theory at low energies.   This procedure is exact when the number of resonances is finite, and otherwise approximate.  Our results are derived from a new class of analytic dispersion relations that depend nonlinearly on the scattering amplitude and apply to an exceedingly broad class of theories and kinematics.
L000177	
L000178	\end{abstract}
L000179	
L000180	
L000181	
L000182	
L000183	\maketitle
L000184	
L000185	
L000186	\mysec{Introduction}The space of effective field theories (EFTs) is infinite. This vast freedom is parameterized by an unending sequence of Wilson coefficients encoding the strengths of all possible low-energy interactions. At the same time, our actual world is dictated by some fixed set of fundamental laws, so physics at the very shortest distances must be specified by a single set of equations. Given knowledge of this complete description, a mechanical but laborious calculation then fixes the precise values of the Wilson coefficients in the low-energy EFT.  A natural question then arises: can this mapping be inverted?  We dub this challenge the {\it EFT inverse problem}.
L000187	
L000188	In this paper we show how the ultraviolet spectrum of particles is subtly encrypted within the Wilson coefficients of the EFT.  We then present a simple algorithm for reconstructing the full dynamics of a tree-level ultraviolet completion---spectrum, scattering amplitudes, and all---directly from those Wilson coefficients.
L000189	
L000190	
L000191	To be very concrete, let us consider an arbitrary tree-level quantum field theory (QFT) composed of a finite number of particles.  The corresponding amplitudes are rational functions of kinematic invariants whose simple poles describe the full spectrum of the theory into the ultraviolet.  The low-energy expansion of these functions define the Wilson coefficients of the corresponding EFT.
L000192	Since any rational function is fixed by a finite number of evaluations, a finite number of Wilson coefficients in the EFT should encode the very same data.
L000193	Here we derive a universal algorithm that achieves precisely this feat, distilling the ultraviolet structure directly from infrared:
L000194	\begin{itemize}
L000195	
L000196	\item[{\it i})] Expand the log derivative of the EFT amplitude,
L000197	\eq{
L000198	 \frac{d}{ds} \log A(s) = \sum_{k}^\infty c_k s^k,
L000199	 }{}
L000200	
L000201	\item[{\it ii})] Build a matrix from these EFT coefficients,
L000202	\eq{
L000203	\mathbf{c}_r =  \left(
L000204	\begin{array}{cccc}
L000205	c_{r} & c_{r+1} & c_{r+2} & \cdots  \\
L000206	c_{r+1} & c_{r+2} & c_{r+3}  &\\
L000207	c_{r+2} & c_{r+3}  & c_{r+4} &  \\
L000208	\vdots &  &  &\ddots \\
L000209	\end{array}
L000210	\right).
L000211	}{cr}
L000212	
L000213	\item[{\it iii})] Count the poles and zeros using the rank,
L000214	\eq{
L000215	\textrm{rank}(\mathbf{c}_r) =d= d_{\rm poles} + d_{\rm zeros}.
L000216	}{rank_condition}
L000217	
L000218	\item[{\it iv})] Determine the locations of these poles and zeros,
L000219	\eq{
L000220	\det\left({\mathbf{c}^{(d)}_{r+1}} - \lambda \mathbf{c}_{r}^{(d)}\right)  =0.
L000221	}{char_eq}
L000222	
L000223	\end{itemize}
L000224	Here, $s$  is the squared center-of-mass energy, $d$ counts poles and zeros of $A(s)$, and $\mathbf{c}_r^{(d)}$ is the $d\times d$ principal submatrix of the $\infty\times \infty$ matrix  $\mathbf{c}_r$.
L000225	
L000226	
L000227	 Steps {\it i}) and {\it ii}) collate the Wilson coefficients of the EFT into the Hankel matrix  $\mathbf{c}_r$.  While this object is formally infinite, step ${\it iii})$ indicates that it actually has finite rank equal to $d$.  This offers a wonderfully practical litmus test for deducing $d$ explicitly.  In particular,
L000228	simply tabulate more and more Wilson coefficients, building up the $\ell\times \ell$ leading principal submatrix $\mathbf{c}_r^{(\ell)}$ for incrementally increasing values of $\ell$.  At each value of $\ell$, compute the determinant of $\mathbf{c}_r^{(\ell)}$.  Once that determinant vanishes, we know that $\ell=d+1$ and we have determined the number of poles and zeros.
L000229	Afterwards, solve the simple characteristic equation in step {\it iv}) for $\lambda$, which as we will show satisfies
L000230	\eq{
L000231	1/\lambda \;=\; \textrm{locations of poles and zeros},
L000232	}{}
L000233	thereby dictating the spectrum as well as the entire functional form of the amplitude deep into the ultraviolet.
L000234	Of course, there is a long history of studying the Wilson coefficients of EFTs~\cite{Wan:2024eto,Arkani-Hamed:2020blm,Bellazzini:2020cot} in terms of the mathematics of the moment problem~\cite{schmudgen2017moment}.  The new element here is that our dispersion relation maps the poles and zeros of the amplitude to this moment sequence directly. All dependence on the residues has been sequestered into the locations of the zeros, and the determination of the spectrum has been transformed into finding the solutions of the generalized eigenvalue problem in Eq.~\eqref{char_eq}.
L000235	
L000236	The linchpin of this procedure is a new class of dispersion relations that are {\it nonlinear} in the scattering amplitude.  Our only assumptions are that the amplitude is meromorphic and that its logarithm is polynomially bounded, so our construction is exceedingly insensitive to ultraviolet behavior.   It can thus be applied  where conventional methods fail, for instance in the kinematic regime of {\it hard scattering} or in theories that are {\it nonpolynomially bounded} in energy.
L000237	As outlined above, our approach is maximally powerful for QFTs with finite numbers of degrees of freedom but also generalizes to
L000238	 theories with infinite towers of particles, like string theory.
L000239	
L000240	
L000241	
L000242	
L000243	
L000244	
L000245	\medskip
L000246	\mysec{Dispersion Relation}The central object in our construction is the quotient,
L000247	\eq{
L000248	Q(s) = \frac{d}{d  s} \log A(s)  =  \frac{A'(s) }{A(s)},
L000249	}{}
L000250	where $A(s)$ is the four-point tree-level amplitude, suppressing all other kinematic dependences.
L000251	
L000252	Clearly $Q(s)$ exhibits poles when $A'(s)$ is singular or $A(s)$ is vanishing.  At the locus of a pole in $A(s)$ at $s=\mu$, the amplitude and its derivative satisfy
L000253	$\lim_{s\rightarrow \mu} A(s)= -R/(s-\mu)+\cdots$
L000254	and $\lim_{s\rightarrow \mu} A'(s) = R/(s-\mu)^2 +\cdots$,
L000255	and similarly at the locus of a zero at $s=\rho$, $\lim_{s\rightarrow \rho} A(s) = S(s-\rho)+\cdots$ and $\lim_{s\rightarrow \rho} A'(s) = S+\cdots$.
L000256	Observe that the residue $R$ and slope $S$ each exactly cancel, so~\footnote{Note that this sum runs over all poles and zeros in the variable $s$ in the amplitude.  Such poles can even arise from long range force carriers in the EFT: for instance, massless exchanges in the $s$ channel, which generate $-1/s$, or massless exchanges in the $t$ channel at fixed $u$, which generate $1/(s+u)$. }
L000257	\eq{
L000258	Q(s) = -\sum_{\rm poles} \frac{1}{s-\mu}+\sum_{\rm zeros} \frac{1}{s-\rho} +\cdots,
L000259	}{Q_poles}
L000260	where the ellipses denote terms that are entire in $s$.  Something a bit miraculous has happened~\footnote{See Ref.~\cite{Remmen:2021zmc} for an analogous construction of an amplitude-like object from the logarithmic derivative of the Landau-Riemann $\Xi$ function, encoding the locations of the zeta function's zeros. }: all information except the locations of the poles and zeros has evaporated from singular components of $Q(s)$!
L000261	
L000262	The simple structure of \Eq{Q_poles} motivates us to consider a dispersion relation for $Q(s)$.  Importantly, $Q(s)$ is calculable within the low-energy expansion of the EFT,
L000263	\eq{
L000264	Q(s) = \frac{d}{ds} \log A(s) = \sum_{k}^\infty c_k s^k.
L000265	}{Q_def}
L000266	whose series coefficients $c_k$ will play a crucial role for us.
L000267	While the Wilson coefficients one would measure experimentally are those in the expansion of $A(s)$ rather than $Q(s)$, the coefficients of these expansions are of course bijectively related, with a closed form given in App.~\hyperref[app:AQ]{A}; we will therefore view the $c_k$ themselves as equivalent to Wilson coefficients.
L000268	Here the lower limit on $k$ can be negative if there are long range forces.
L000269	We can then relate $c_k$ to the ultraviolet dynamics via a contour integral about the origin of the complex $s$ plane,
L000270	\eq{\hspace{-1mm}
L000271	c_k&= \frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint\limits_{s=0} \frac{ds}{s^{1+k}} Q(s)\\
L000272	 &= {-} \frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint\limits_{s\neq 0}  \frac{ds}{s^{1+k}} \left(  {-}\sum_{\rm poles} \frac{1}{s\,{-}\,\mu}\,{+}\,\sum_{\rm zeros} \frac{1}{s\,{-}\,\rho} \right),
L000273	}{}
L000274	where $k$ defines the degree of the subtraction.  Evaluating the contour integral on the right-hand side yields a generalized version of Cauchy's argument principle~\footnote{Cauchy's argument principle states that
L000275	\begin{equation*}
L000276	\qquad\;\;\frac{1}{2\pi i}\protect\oint_\gamma \frac{f'(z)}{f(z)}\,dz = d_{\rm zeros} - d_{\rm poles},
L000277	\end{equation*}
L000278	where $f(z)$ is meromorphic and $\gamma$ is a closed curve encircling $d_{\rm zeros}$ zeros and $d_{\rm poles}$ poles of $f(z)$.
L000279	}
L000280	\eq{
L000281	c_k&= \sum_{\rm poles} \frac{1}{\mu^{1+k}} - \sum_{\rm zeros} \frac{1}{\rho^{1+k}}  .
L000282	}{ck}
L000283	Physically, this expression implies our central result that the Wilson coefficients of the EFT directly encode the locations of poles and zeros of the amplitude~\footnote{A higher-degree zero can be accommodated by allowing $\rho$ to run over repeated elements or equivalently by rescaling the corresponding term in \Eq{ck} by the degree of degeneracy.  The same applies for poles, though higher-degree poles are forbidden by locality.}.
L000284	
L000285	In the above equations we have dropped boundary terms at complex infinity for $s$.  This is justified if
L000286	\eq{
L000287	\lim_{s\rightarrow \infty} \frac{Q(s)}{s^k} =0,
L000288	}{}
L000289	for our chosen value of $k$.  We emphasize that this condition is typically {\it exceedingly weak},
L000290	since the log of the amplitude in all known cases---including all QFTs and string theories---grows at worst as a polynomial in $s$.
L000291	This criterion only fails if the high-energy behavior of the amplitude is {\it doubly exponential} in $s$.
L000292	
L000293	\medskip
L000294	\mysec{Field Theory Examples}It will be illuminating to explore the implications of \Eq{ck} in various QFTs.
L000295	
L000296	To begin, let us consider a toy model describing the exchange of a single particle of mass squared $\mu$ in the $s$ channel.  The corresponding amplitude is
L000297	\eq{
L000298	A(s) = -\frac{R}{s-\mu}.
L000299	}{}
L000300	Since the quotient $Q(s)$ goes to zero at large $s$, we can consider any $k\geq 0$.  From \Eq{ck}, we have
L000301	$c_k =1/\mu^{1+k}$,
L000302	which confirms the obvious expectation that the Wilson coefficients are infinitely redundant and just reconstruct the geometric series induced by the $s$ channel exchange.
L000303	
L000304	For a theory with two exchanges in the $s$ channel, the story is essentially the same.  The amplitude is
L000305	\eq{
L000306	A(s) = -\frac{R_1}{s-\mu_1}-\frac{R_2}{s-\mu_2},
L000307	}{}
L000308	and \Eq{ck}  confirms that
L000309	\eq{
L000310	c_k &= \frac{1}{\mu_1^{1+k}} + \frac{1}{\mu_2^{1+k}} - \frac{1}{\rho^{1+k}} ,
L000311	}{}
L000312	where the single zero is $\rho =  (R_1 \mu_2 + R_2 \mu_1)/(R_1+R_2)$.
L000313	
L000314	In general, the amplitude for any tree-level, polynomially-bounded QFT takes the form
L000315	\eq{
L000316	A(s) \propto \prod\limits_{n=1}^{d_{\rm zeros}} (s-\rho_n) \Big/ \prod\limits_{n=1}^{d_{\rm poles}}(s-\mu_n).
L000317	}{A_rational}
L000318	Here all kinematic invariants other than $s$ have been fixed in such a way that the resulting amplitude is meromorphic in $s$.  This would occur, for instance, at fixed $t$.
L000319	By inserting arbitrary values of $\mu_n$ and $\rho_n$ into  \Eq{A_rational}, computing $Q(s)$, and expanding as in \Eq{Q_def}, we precisely confirm Eq.~\eqref{ck}.
L000320	
L000321	
L000322	\medskip
L000323	\mysec{String Theory Examples}Next, let us confirm our result in \Eq{ck} for the case of the Veneziano amplitude for the scattering of open strings~\cite{Veneziano}.  In the context of the superstring, this amplitude is
L000324	\eq{
L000325	A(s,t) &= -\frac{\Gamma(-s)\Gamma(-t)}{\Gamma(1-s-t)} ,
L000326	}{A_Ven}
L000327	where for simplicity we have stripped away the gauge invariant prefactor that encodes the external polarization data. It will be instructive to study our results for various choices of kinematics.
L000328	
L000329	\smallskip
L000330	\noindent {\it Fixed $t$.} Consider the Regge limit of fixed $t$ and large $s$.  Inserting \Eq{A_Ven} into \Eq{Q_def}, we obtain
L000331	\eq{\hspace{-1mm}
L000332	Q(s) =&\, \frac{d}{ds} \log A(s,t) \\
L000333	=& -\frac{1}{s} + \gamma +\psi(1\,{-}\,t) + \left(\frac{\pi^2}{6} \,{-}\, \psi^{(1)}(1\,{-}\,t) \right)s\\
L000334	&+ \left(\zeta(3) \,{+}\, \frac12  \psi^{(2)}(1\,{-}\,t) \right)s^2 +\cdots.
L000335	}{Q_Ven}
L000336	Since the Veneziano amplitude in Eq.~\eqref{A_Ven} has poles at $s=n$ and zeros at $s=1+n-t$, we find that the terms in Eq.~\eqref{Q_Ven} agree exactly with \Eq{ck},
L000337	\eq{
L000338	c_k^{(t)}(t) &= \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{n^{1+k}} -\sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{1}{(1+n-t)^{1+k}} \\
L000339	&= \zeta(1+k) - \zeta(1+k,1-t)
L000340	}{eq:ckstring}
L000341	after analytic resummation, since the Hurwitz zeta function satisfies $\zeta(1+k,1-t) = (-1)^{k+1} \psi^{(k)}(1-t)/k!$~\footnote{For $k=0$ and $-1$, this sum must of course be handled with care. }.
L000342	
L000343	\smallskip
L000344	\noindent {\it Fixed $t/s$.} Conventional dispersion relations fail for {\it hard scattering} in the context of higher-spin towers because the corresponding amplitudes grow as~\cite{Caron-Huot:2016icg}
L000345	\eq{
L000346	\lim_{s,t\rightarrow+\infty}A(s,t) \sim e^{B(s,t)} ,
L000347	}{}
L000348	where we have defined
L000349	\eq{
L000350	B(s,t) = (s\,{+}\,t)\log(s\,{+}\,t) -s\log s - t \log t +\cdots,
L000351	}{}
L000352	which is familiar from string amplitudes.  This exponential blowup stymies conventional dispersion relations by introducing uncontrolled boundary contributions.
L000353	
L000354	In contrast, our new dispersion relations are perfectly amenable to hard scattering.  Given the fixed ratio,
L000355	\eq{
L000356	z=t/s,
L000357	}{eq:zratio}
L000358	we have $B(s,z) =[(1+z)\log(1+z)-z \log z]s+\cdots$,
L000359	 corresponding to a constant quotient,
L000360	 \eq{
L000361	\lim_{s\rightarrow \infty} Q(s) = (1+z)\log(1+z)-z \log z.
L000362	}{}
L000363	Thus, our dispersion relations are applicable for $k\geq 1$.  Expanding the Veneziano amplitude at fixed $z$, we obtain
L000364	\eq{
L000365	Q(s) =& \frac{d}{ds} \log A(s,zs) \\
L000366	=& -\frac{2}{s} -\frac{\pi^2z }{3}s -3\zeta(3) z(1+z) s^2 + \cdots.
L000367	}{eq:Qz}
L000368	With the choice in Eq.~\eqref{eq:zratio}, the Veneziano amplitude in Eq.~\eqref{A_Ven} has poles at $s=n$ and $n/z$, as well as zeros at $s=(1+n)/(1+z)$, giving us the prediction of \Eq{ck},
L000369	\eq{
L000370	c_k &= \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{n^{1+k}} +\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{\left(\frac{n}{z}\right)^{1+k}}-\sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{1}{\left(\frac{1+n}{1+z}\right)^{1+k}}\\
L000371	&= \zeta(1+k) \left(1+z^{1+k} -(1+z)^{1+k} \right).
L000372	}{}
L000373	Comparing with Eq.~\eqref{eq:Qz}, we find complete agreement.
L000374	
L000375	
L000376	\smallskip
L000377	\noindent {\it Fixed $u$.} The case of fixed $u$ is especially interesting. At fixed $u$, the string amplitude asymptotes to
L000378	\eq{
L000379	\lim_{s\rightarrow \infty} A(s,u) \sim  s^{u-1}.
L000380	}{}
L000381	For general fixed $u$, we compute the quotient
L000382	\eq{
L000383	Q(s)  &= \frac{d}{ds} \log A(s,-s-u) \\
L000384	=& -\frac{1}{s} + \gamma +\psi(u) + \left(\frac{\pi^2}{6} + \psi^{(1)}(u) \right)s\\
L000385	&+ \left(\zeta(3) + \frac12  \psi^{(2)}(u) \right)s^2 +\cdots ,
L000386	}{eq:Qu}
L000387	At fixed $u$, the Veneziano amplitude has poles at $s=n$ and $s=-n -u$. While the Veneziano amplitude has ``hidden zeros'' at negative integer $u$ that generalize at higher multiplicity~\cite{Arkani-Hamed:2023swr}, there are otherwise no zeros in $s$ at fixed $u$.
L000388	Again computing the prediction of Eq.~\eqref{ck},
L000389	\eq{
L000390	c_k^{(u)}(u) &= \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{n^{1+k}} +\sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{1}{(-n-u)^{1+k}} \\
L000391	&= \zeta(1+k) + (-1)^{k+1} \zeta(1+k,u),
L000392	}{cku}
L000393	we find agreement with Eq.~\eqref{eq:Qu}.
L000394	Interestingly, for $u=0$, the Wilson coefficients $c_k$ are zero for even $k$ and nonnegative for odd $k$, irrespective of unitarity.
L000395	
L000396	As is well known, the EFT coefficients in the expansion of the Veneziano amplitude in Eq.~\eqref{A_Ven} are multiple zeta values expressed as an exponential sum over simple zeta values~\cite{Schlotterer2012,green2019}. Our dispersion relation for the quotient $Q(s)$ provides an alternative understanding of this behavior.
L000397	
L000398	\medskip
L000399	\mysec{Spectroscopy}
L000400	The above examples illustrate how the poles and zeros of the amplitude exactly fix the coefficients $c_k$.  Here we will see that the converse is also true: the Wilson coefficients can be manipulated to extract the spectrum of poles and zeros.
L000401	
L000402	
L000403	Consider a general QFT amplitude, which is polynomially bounded and exhibits a finite number of poles and zeros. Our results imply that the $c_k$ are given as moment sums over the poles and zeros of $A(s)$ as in Eq.~\eqref{ck}.
L000404	It will be convenient to repackage this sum as
L000405	\eq{
L000406	c_k &= \sum_{n=1}^d \sigma_n \lambda_n^{1+k}.
L000407	}{eq:ckd}
L000408	Here $d=d_{\rm poles}+d_{\rm zeros}$, and $\sigma_n= +1$ or $-1$ when $1/\lambda_n$ is a pole or a zero, respectively. We consider the case where the $\lambda_n$ are all distinct, though this can be generalized~\footnote{In the case of higher-degree zeros, we keep the $\lambda_n$ distinct but absorb the degree of degeneracy into the magnitude of $\sigma_n$.
L000409	Meanwhile, if a pole and a zero happen to coincide, then their contributions cancel in $c_k$, the associated $\sigma_n$ effectively vanishes, and the corresponding $\lambda_n$ can be excluded from the sum.
L000410	In such cases, the Vandermonde matrix $[\boldsymbol{\lambda}]_{in} = \lambda_n^i$ remains left-invertible.}. The Hankel matrix in \Eq{cr} becomes
L000411	\eq{
L000412	[\mathbf{c}_{r}]_{ij} = c_{r+i+j} = \sum_{n=1}^d \sigma_n \lambda_n^{1+r+i+j}.
L000413	}{}
L000414	Formally, the $i$ and $j$ indices extend infinitely, since there is an infinite sequence of Wilson coefficients in the EFT.
L000415	By inspection, we see that $\mathbf{c}_{r}$ factorizes into
L000416	\eq{
L000417	\mathbf{c}_{r} = \boldsymbol{\lambda}\cdot \mathbf{d}_{r}\cdot \boldsymbol{\lambda}^T,
L000418	}{eq:ctransform}
L000419	where we have defined the $\infty \times d$ Vandermonde matrix $[\boldsymbol{\lambda}]_{in} = \lambda_n^i$ and the $d\times d$ diagonal matrix
L000420	\be
L000421	[\mathbf{d}_{r}]_{nm} = \sigma_n \lambda_n^{1+r}  \delta_{nm}.\label{eq:dmat}
L000422	\ee
L000423	Since the $\lambda_n$ are by definition distinct, $\boldsymbol{\lambda}$ is left-invertible. The Vandermonde decomposition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ctransform} preserves rank, so $\mathbf{c}_{r}$ has rank $d$, confirming \Eq{rank_condition}.
L000424	
L000425	A corollary of this logic is that the $\ell\times\ell$ leading principal submatrix satisfies
L000426	$\textrm{rank}(\boldsymbol{c}_r^{(\ell)}) = \min(\ell,d)$
L000427	which reduces to \Eq{rank_condition} when $\ell$ is infinite.  This observation implies that the determinant of the matrix is zero beyond a critical value,
L000428	\eq{
L000429	\det(\boldsymbol{c}_r^{(\ell)}) = 0 \quad \textrm{for} \quad \ell > d.
L000430	}{}
L000431	Then $d$ is determined by populating the matrix $\boldsymbol{c}_r^{(\ell)}$ until $\ell$ is sufficiently large that the determinant vanishes.
L000432	
L000433	To enumerate these quantities, we solve the characteristic equation in \Eq{char_eq} for $\lambda$.  The argument of the determinant in \Eq{char_eq} is the $d\times d$ submatrix of $\mathbf{c}_{r+1}-\lambda \mathbf{c}_{r} = \boldsymbol{\lambda}\cdot (\mathbf{d}_{r+1}-\lambda \mathbf{d}_{r})\cdot \boldsymbol{\lambda}^T$.
L000434	Rewriting the diagonal matrix in the center as $[\mathbf{d}_{r+1}- \lambda \mathbf{d}_{r}]_{nm} = \sigma_n \lambda_n^{1+r} (\lambda_n -\lambda) \delta_{nm}$,
L000435	we see that zeros occur precisely when $\lambda = \lambda_n$.
L000436	The solutions of \Eq{char_eq} thus define the entire sequence $\lambda_n$. Inverting the Vandermonde matrix, we have $\mathbf{d}_{r}= \boldsymbol{\lambda}^{-1}\cdot \mathbf{c}_{r} \cdot (\boldsymbol{\lambda}^T)^{-1}$. For odd $r$, we see from Eq.~\eqref{eq:dmat} that the eigenvalues of this matrix precisely dictate $\sigma_n$, which flags whether $1/\lambda_n$ is a pole or a zero.
L000437	We thus find that
L000438	\eq{
L000439	\sigma_n = {\rm sign}([\mathbf{d}_{r}]_{nn})  \textrm{ for odd } r.
L000440	}{eq:sigmasign}
L000441	Since we can always choose odd $r$,
L000442	this final datum allows us to determine the exact positions of all poles and zeros.
L000443	
L000444	\begin{figure}[t]
L000445	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{strings.pdf}
L000446	\caption{The spectrum of string theory is extracted directly from its low-energy EFT.  Given the Wilson coefficients $c_k$ in  \Eq{cku} for $u=0$, construct the $\ell \times \ell$ principal submatrix $\boldsymbol{c}_r^{(\ell)}$, whose entries are $[\mathbf{c}_{r}^{(\ell)}]_{ij} = c_{r+i+j}$.  Solving $\det({\boldsymbol{c}}_{r+1}^{(\ell)} -  \lambda^{(\ell)} {\boldsymbol{c}}_r^{(\ell)}) = 0$ for $r=1$, then plot $1/ \lambda^{(\ell)}$ as a function of $\ell$.   The spectrum approaches that of the string for large $\ell$.}
L000447	\label{fig:strings}
L000448	\end{figure}
L000449	
L000450	In summary, to extract the complete spectrum of poles and zeros from the EFT coefficients,  solve for $\lambda$ in \Eq{char_eq} to extract all $\lambda_n$.  Then insert $\lambda_n$ into the definition of $\boldsymbol{\lambda}$ to define the matrix ${\bf d}_r$, which determines the sign of $\sigma_n$ from Eq.~\eqref{eq:sigmasign} and specifies $1/\lambda_n$ is a pole or a zero.  While this procedure takes as input the Wilson coefficient expansion of $Q(s)$, the very same algorithm applies to the coefficients of $A(s)$, in which case $\lambda_n$ and $\sigma_n$ in \Eq{eq:dmat} are the mass and residue at each pole.
L000451	
L000452	The above procedure works beautifully if we have access to a sufficient number of Wilson coefficients.  Since the Hankel matrix $\boldsymbol{c}_{r}$ has rank $d$, we need all Wilson coefficients in the leading $d \times d$ principal submatrix $\boldsymbol{c}_{r}^{(d)}$.  But what if we have insufficient data?  Concretely, consider the case where we are privy only to the Wilson coefficients in the leading $\ell\times \ell$ principal submatrix $\boldsymbol{c}_{r}^{(\ell)}$ for $\ell < d$.  Notably, this case subsumes that of string theory, whose infinite spectrum implies $d=\infty$.
L000453	The naturally truncated version of \Eq{char_eq} is then~\footnote{A similar mathematical construction was used in Ref.~\cite{Bellazzini:2020cot} to extract the spectrum, albeit in the context of scattering in the forward limit.  In the present work, we directly extract both the poles and zeros of the amplitude at general kinematics.}
L000454	\eq{
L000455	\det\left({\boldsymbol{c}}_{r+1}^{(\ell)} -  \lambda^{(\ell)} {\boldsymbol{c}}_r^{(\ell)}\right) = 0 \quad \textrm{for} \quad \ell <d.
L000456	}{char_eq_trunc}
L000457	As shown in App.~\hyperref[pade]{B}, while $1/\lambda$ labels the poles and zeros of $Q(s)$, the quantity $1/\lambda^{(\ell)}$ labels the poles and zeros of the best-fit rational polynomial for $Q(s)$, known as a Pad\'e approximant.  Even for an infinite spectrum, the convergence properties of Pad\'e approximants~\cite{Nuttall} imply the convergence of $1/ \lambda^{(\ell)}$ to the exact locations of the poles and zeros as $\ell\rightarrow\infty$.
L000458	See \Fig{fig:strings} for the approximate string spectrum extracted from the Wilson coefficients of the Veneziano amplitude in \Eq{A_Ven} for $t=-s$.
L000459	
L000460	\medskip
L000461	\mysec{Double Copy}Conventional dispersion relations are linear in the amplitude, so they act simply on {\it sums} of amplitudes.  In contrast, our new dispersion relations are logarithmic, so they play well with {\it products}.  A context where such products arise is the double copy~\cite{Bern:2019prr}.  In particular, the KLT relations~\cite{KLT} recast the Virasoro-Shapiro amplitude~\cite{Virasoro,Shapiro} of closed strings as
L000462	\eq{
L000463	A^{(\rm VS)}(s,t)&= -\frac{\Gamma(-s)\Gamma(-t)\Gamma(-u)}{\Gamma(1+s)\Gamma(1+t)\Gamma(1+u)} \\
L000464	&=\frac{\sin \pi s}{\pi}\,A^{(\rm V)}(s,t)\,A^{(\rm V)}(s,u),
L000465	}{klt}
L000466	where $A^{(\rm V)}(s,t)$ is the Veneziano amplitude defined in \Eq{A_Ven}.
L000467	Inserting \Eq{klt} into  our dispersion relation, we discover a subtle link between the Wilson coefficients of the open and closed string,
L000468	\eq{
L000469	c_k^{(\mathrm{VS})}(t)=c_k^{(t)}(t)+c_k^{(u)}(t)+c_k^{(\mathrm{KLT})}.
L000470	}{ckKLT}
L000471	where $c_k^{(t)}$ and $c_k^{(u)}$ are defined in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:ckstring} and \eqref{cku}.
L000472	Here $c_k^{(\mathrm{KLT})}$ are new terms that arise in the dispersion relation because of zeros in the KLT kernel and can be given in terms of the Bernoulli numbers,
L000473	\eq{
L000474	\frac{d}{ds} \log \sin \pi s
L000475	& =
L000476	\frac{1}{s}
L000477	+
L000478	\sum_{k\,\mathrm{odd}}
L000479	\frac{(-1)^{\frac{k+1}{2}}(2\pi)^{k+1}}{(k+1)!}\,B_{k+1}\,s^k \\ &
L000480	=
L000481	\frac{1}{s}
L000482	+
L000483	\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} c_k^{(\mathrm{KLT})}\,s^k.
L000484	}{}
L000485	We have verified \Eq{ckKLT} using the Wilson coefficients for the Veneziano and Virasoro-Shapiro amplitudes.
L000486	
L000487	
L000488	\medskip
L000489	\mysec{Discussion}This work offers numerous avenues for future inquiry.
L000490	A natural question regards  the inclusion of spin.  For simplicity we have  focused entirely on scattering processes with external scalars only, but extending to general spin should be straightforward.  In particular, one can strip off all helicity weights at will and apply our methodology to the multiplicative remainders.  For example, given a four-dimensional scattering amplitude of fermions, $A(s,t) = \langle 12\rangle [34] f(s,t)$, we would derive dispersion relations for $f(s,t)$.   While this approach implicitly drops helicity-dependent zeros, another alternative would be to instead manipulate the analytic continuation of the {\it squared amplitude}, $|A(s,t)|^2 = s^2 f(s,t)^2$, which carries no helicity weight whatsoever.
L000491	
L000492	
L000493	Our results should generalize to tree-level amplitudes at higher multiplicity.  In particular, \Eq{ck} applies equally well to higher-point amplitudes, provided we define the variable $s$ in the dispersion relation as some higher-point kinematic invariant.  We have verified by explicit calculation that  the analogue of \Eq{ck} holds for five-point amplitudes in planar $\phi^3$ theory and six-point amplitudes in the nonlinear sigma model.
L000494	
L000495	
L000496	On the other hand, a clear limitation of our current framework is the necessity of the tree-level approximation. Since the mechanics of our dispersion relations depend so crucially on the meromorphic structure of tree amplitudes, the extension to loop level is far from clear.  Typical loop amplitudes exhibit  logarithms and polylogarithms, with rare exceptions such as the rational loop amplitudes of Yang-Mills theory~\cite{Henn_2020}  and self-dual gravity~\cite{Bern_1998}, which preserve meromorphicity. We leave this important question to future work.
