L000514	\mysec{Appendix A: From $A(s)$ to $Q(s)$}\label{app:AQ}The series expansion of $Q(s)= d \log A(s)/ds$ encodes that exact same information as the series expansion for $A(s)$.
L000515	However, any experimental probe will reconstruct the low-energy expansion of $A(s)$ rather than $Q(s)$.
L000516	For completeness, we now derive a simple formula relating the series expansions of these functions.
L000517	In particular, let us define the EFT expansion of the amplitude,
L000518	\be
L000519	A(s) = \sum_{j=0}^\infty b_j s^j,
L000520	\ee
L000521	for convenience considering the case without a massless pole, since the generalization is straightforward. By explicit calculation, we find that the expansion of $Q(s)$ can be written in terms of the expansion of $A(s)$ as
L000522	\be
L000523	\begin{aligned}
L000524	c_0 &= \frac{b_1}{b_0} \\
L000525	c_1 &= -\frac{b_1^2 - 2b_0 b_2}{b_0^2} \\
L000526	c_2 &= \frac{b_1^3 -3 b_0 b_1 b_2 + 3b_0^2 b_3}{b_0^3}  \\
L000527	c_3 &= -\frac{b_1^4 - 4b_0 b_1^2 b_2 + 2b_0^2 b_2^2 + 4 b_0^2 b_1 b_3 -4 b_0^3 b_4}{b_0^4},
L000528	\end{aligned}
L000529	\ee
L000530	and so on.
L000531	The numerical coefficients appearing in these equations coincide with those of the multivariable Faber partition polynomials.  In fact, one can find an explicit relation between $b_j$  and $c_k$, given by
L000532	\be
L000533	\hspace{-1mm} c_k = -(k\,{+}\,1) \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \sum_{\substack{m_i \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\\ \sum_{i=1}^{k+1} i m_i = k+1}} \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\Gamma({\textstyle\sum_{l=1}^{k+1}m_l}) \prod_{j=1}^{k+1} \frac{(-b_j/b_0)^{m_j}}{m_j!},\hspace{-1mm}
L000534	\ee
L000535	where the right-hand side sums over partitions of $k\,{+}\,1$.
L000536	Importantly, any $c_k$ only depends on $b_j$ for $j\leq k\,{+}\,1$.  Reconstruction of the spectrum of poles and zeros requires knowledge of some leading set of the $c_k$, which translates into knowledge of some leading set of the $b_j$ and so might in principle be accessed experimentally.
L000537	
